s("What are my options for selling a house fast in Cornelius, NC?",
"<p>Cornelius homeowners can list the house, sell it themselves, or accept a cash offer from a buyer who takes it as-is. The right pick depends on how soon you need to close and how much preparation the house needs.</p>"
"<p>With a median owner-occupied home value of $559,400 on the Census figures, the stakes of the choice are high. A listing asks for staging, photos and showings, then a wait for a buyer's financing. A cash sale skips most of that and can close on a date you choose.</p>"
"<ul><li>Listing suits a move-in-ready home when your timeline is flexible.</li>"
"<li>A self-sale saves commission but leaves contracts, disclosures and negotiation with you.</li>"
"<li>A cash offer suits inherited houses, houses with deferred repairs, and owners facing a deadline.</li></ul>"
"<p>Any deed you sign is recorded with the Mecklenburg County Register of Deeds, and under North Carolina law a conveyance is not valid against lien creditors or later buyers for value until it is registered in the county where the land lies.</p>")
s("What should a seller know about the town of Cornelius?",
"<p>Cornelius is a Lake Norman town in northern Mecklenburg County with 35,094 residents by the Census Bureau's July 1, 2025 estimate. The town says it was founded in 1893 and incorporated on March 4, 1905, making it the second youngest of the county's six incorporated towns.</p>"
"<p>The town traces its origin to a dispute over cotton weighing in neighboring Davidson. One of the two Davidson cotton firms moved just south of the line, later built a mill with money from Joseph Benjamin Cornelius, and the town took his name. Duke Power created Lake Norman in 1963, and the town says that gave it about 70 miles of lakeside property. Cornelius also lists ten public parks and a championship golf course.</p>"
"<table><caption>Cornelius town figures from Census QuickFacts</caption><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Cornelius figure</th></tr></thead><tbody>"
"<tr><td>Population estimate, July 1, 2025</td><td>35,094</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Population, April 1, 2010 Census</td><td>24,866</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Persons 65 and over</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Owner-occupied housing unit rate, 2020-2024</td><td>69.5%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Median value of owner-occupied homes, 2020-2024</td><td>$559,400</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Median gross rent, 2020-2024</td><td>$1,722</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Median household income, 2020-2024</td><td>$113,767</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Land area, 2020</td><td>12.64 square miles</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>One in five residents is 65 or older, a share that explains why the state's age-based tax relief matters to many Cornelius sellers and heirs.</p>")
s("How does Mecklenburg County value a Cornelius home for tax?",
"<p>Mecklenburg County values every property, and the town board and county commissioners each set a rate. County tax staff state that revaluation determines market value while elected boards decide the rates, and the value times the rate gives the bill.</p>"
"<p>The county revalues every four years, and its tax office says the next revaluation takes place in 2027. Values are set as of January 1, 2027, the uniform schedule of values went to the county commissioners on September 1, 2026, and new notices go out in early 2027. After a revaluation, the county says rates are adjusted to stay revenue neutral unless elected officials choose otherwise.</p>"
"<p>If you list a house in the months around that change, a buyer will ask how the new value could affect the bill. Point them to the county's online property record, and tell them the town sets its own rate separately from the county's.</p>"
"<ul><li>Informal review: request it within 30 days of the date on your notice.</li>"
"<li>Formal appeal: present it to the Board of Equalization and Review.</li>"
"<li>Next level: the state Property Tax Commission, with notice due within 30 days after the board mails its decision.</li></ul>")
s("When are Cornelius property taxes due, and what do late payments cost?",
"<p>North Carolina makes the tax due September 1 of the fiscal year it covers. If you pay before January 6, there is no interest, and from January 6 onward interest of 2 percent applies through the end of January.</p>"
"<p>From February 1 until the bill is paid, interest runs at three-fourths of one percent a month or fraction of a month. A county or town may adopt a discount for early payment by resolution passed by May 1 before the due date, so ask the Mecklenburg tax office whether one applies. Mailed payments count on the USPS postmark date.</p>"
"<p>The tax lien attaches to the parcel as of the January listing date. Before closing, ask the Mecklenburg County tax collector for the status of the current bill so the closing attorney can settle it from your proceeds.</p>")
s("Which relief programs can reduce taxes for a Cornelius owner?",
"<p>Three state programs exist, and an owner can use only one of them. All three are filed with the county assessor, not the Department of Revenue.</p>"
"<ul><li>Elderly or disabled exclusion: the greater of $25,000 or half of the home's appraised value. The NCDOR form prints the 2026 income limit as $38,800.</li>"
"<li>Disabled veteran exclusion: the first $45,000 of appraised value, with no age or income limit on the NCDOR form.</li>"
"<li>Circuit breaker: tax limited to 4 percent of income up to the limit and 5 percent above it up to 150 percent of the limit, which the form prints as $58,200 for 2026.</li></ul>"
"<p>Applications are made on Form AV-9, and the county must accept them through June 1 before the tax year. Cornelius has one more local feature: the town's FAQ says a designated historic landmark gets an automatic deferral of 50 percent of ad valorem tax on the designated portions. A structure must be at least 50 years old to start the review, and the Charlotte-Mecklenburg Historic Landmarks Commission decides eligibility.</p>")
s("What does the seller pay at closing in Cornelius?",
"<p>The seller pays the excise tax on the deed to the Register of Deeds before recording. The rate is $1.00 on each $500, or fractional part, of the consideration or value of the interest conveyed.</p>"
"<p>Half goes to the county general fund and half to the state. Recording fees are set statewide at $26.00 for an ordinary instrument of up to 15 pages, plus $4.00 for each further page, and $64.00 for a mortgage or deed of trust up to 35 pages. Recording a satisfaction of an old deed of trust costs nothing.</p>"
"<p>Your mortgage payoff, any unpaid tax and the closing attorney's fee come out of your proceeds as well. Ask the attorney for a settlement statement ahead of time.</p>")
s("What do Cornelius permits and zoning mean for a sale?",
"<p>Building and permanent sign permits go through Mecklenburg County, but the Cornelius Planning Department must approve the zoning first. Check what permits exist on your parcel, because the town's FAQ says unpermitted work must be disclosed when you sell and can raise your taxable square footage.</p>"
"<ul><li>Accessory dwellings: only one per property, in the rear yard, and together with other accessory structures it may not exceed 50 percent of the principal building's footage. Travel trailers and RVs do not qualify.</li>"
"<li>Short-term rentals: called transient occupancy, allowed only in areas shown on the town's Transient Occupancy Map and subject to Chapter 6.2.43 of the Land Development Code.</li>"
"<li>Watershed: most of the town lies in the Lake Norman or Mountain Island watershed, where impervious area limits and water quality buffers can apply.</li></ul>"
"<p>Buyers who want to use a Cornelius house as a rental should check the map before making an offer. Buyers of lakefront parcels should ask about buffers.</p>")
s("What if my Cornelius house is in foreclosure, inherited or tenant-occupied?",
"<p>Each situation has its own state timetable. A cash sale can fit inside most of them, but the dates matter.</p>"
"<ul><li>Foreclosure: the clerk of superior court holds the hearing, notice is served at least 10 days before it, and an upset bid after the sale must top the last bid by at least 5 percent, with a minimum increase of $750.</li>"
"<li>Inherited home: a personal property estate of $20,000 or less may use an affidavit 30 days after death if no one has petitioned for a personal representative. North Carolina has no estate tax on deaths on or after January 1, 2013.</li>"
"<li>Tenants: a month-to-month rental ends on 7 days' notice, and a lease is forfeited if rent is not paid within 10 days of demand.</li></ul>"
"<p>Ask a North Carolina attorney which timetable applies to your documents.</p>")
